{
  "requirement": "The filename-based constructors include Rust, Python, TypeScript, TSX,\nMarkdown, CSS, TOML, and YAML languages. Extension matching is\ncase-insensitive, using each implementation's claimed extensions.\nThe [language chapters](#language-specific-rules) define those mappings.\nA filename without a claimed extension uses the\n[plain-text outline](sources/plain-text.md): a file root with one child per\nnonblank line.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.outlines.defaults`.\n\n**Obligations**\n1. The filename-based constructors include the Rust, Python, TypeScript, TSX, Markdown, CSS, TOML and YAML languages.\n2. Extension matching is case-insensitive and uses each implementation's claimed extensions.\n3. A filename without a claimed extension gets the plain-text outline: a file root with one child per nonblank line.\n\n**Coverage of the bundled languages and extensions**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_languages_claim_extensions_and_supply_the_same_public_outline]` runs a `check` helper once per language: Rust, Python, TypeScript, Tsx, Markdown, Css, Toml and Yaml.\n- For each language it asserts the exact claimed extension list: `rs`, `py`/`pyi`, `ts`/`mts`/`cts`, `tsx`, `md`/`markdown`, `css`, `toml`, and `yml`/`yaml`.\n- It builds content with `from_source` using an uppercase filename such as `a.RS` or `a.PYI`. The result's fingerprint equals the explicit-language outline's fingerprint, and its scheme equals the language's scheme.\n- It loops over every claimed extension in uppercase, using `not-present.<EXT>`. Each one yields the same fingerprint and resolves a language-specific selector such as `fn[check]` or `section[Check]`. This covers case-insensitivity and every claimed extension, including the multi-extension languages.\n- It also checks that `PlainText` claims no extensions.\n\n**Lowercase extensions**\n- Many other tests in `outline_content.rs` use lowercase filenames and depend on the language-specific outline. Examples are `auth.rs`, `headings.md`, `config.toml`, `config.yaml`, `nested.yml`, `decorated.py`, `api.ts`, `api.tsx` and `duplicates.css`.\n- These cover lowercase mapping for every bundled language.\n\n**Plain-text fallback**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[unknown_extensions_keep_nonblank_lines_and_content_selectors]` uses `missing.unclaimed`, an unclaimed extension.\n  - The root selector is empty.\n  - The outline has a root plus one `line` child per nonblank line, and the blank line produces no item.\n  - The spans of the three nonblank lines (`alpha`, `beta`, `alpha`) are asserted.\n- The same test uses `missing` (no extension) with empty text. This gives a root only, with a zero-length span.\n- Other tests also cover the fallback with `note.txt`, `plain`, `unicode.unknown` and `note.unknown`.\n\n**Constructor reach**\n- `from_path` only reads the file and then calls `from_source`. It has no logic of its own for choosing a language.\n- `from_path` is also exercised in `file_constructors_infer_the_language_and_return_addressed_contents`.\n- The implementation (`Registry::for_path`) lowercases the extension and searches the providers' claimed extensions. The tests' expected results come from the requirement text, not from this code.\n\nI found no gap.",
  "references": "d471bd745bc4f594a635a499c6a5b2b2a40410b4e7dc7b8777902cfd64211913",
